WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
The sun’s gone bad! People and&nbsp;
animals are melting everywhere!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:07.040
The world is coming to an end and there’s&nbsp;
nothing I can do about it! Will I be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.720
find food? Will I be able to defeat or avoid&nbsp;
the horrific flesh monsters all around me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.080
or the desperate and hungry survivors left in this&nbsp;
terrible new world? Keep watching and find out.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.480
Can I survive 100 days in&nbsp;
SCP-001 - When Day Breaks!?&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.840
Hey folks, it’s ya boy, Kyle.&nbsp;
You probably know me more for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.160
gaming videos than post-apocalyptic&nbsp;
vlogs, but hey, I’m a versatile guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.800
and I think I might go insane from the fear if I&nbsp;
don’t talk to somebody about all this craziness.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.240
If you’re alive and seeing this right now,&nbsp;
well, congratulations, you’re probably doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.520
a lot better than most people here, if you can&nbsp;
call them “People” now. But if you’re seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.280
this a few years in the future, like, I&nbsp;
don’t know, you woke up from a ten-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:50.000
coma like Rick from The Walking Dead and you’re&nbsp;
wondering what the hell happened to Planet Earth…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.360
This video is probably gonna answer a lot of your&nbsp;
questions. First thing’s first, whatever you do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:01.840
you’ve gotta stay away from the sun. It touches&nbsp;
you even for a second, you’re dead, or worse.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.320
Welcome to Day One of the End of the World.
For all of you who are still in a solid state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.120
of matter, you’re probably wondering how I’m still&nbsp;
alive, too. Chances are, it’s for the exact same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:16.800
reason you are: Sheer, dumb luck. I was down&nbsp;
here in my gaming basement when day broke, just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.760
level grinding when my TV got taken over by those&nbsp;
SCP Foundation people, telling us that the sun’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.240
gone evil for whatever reason and now we’ve all&nbsp;
gotta stay inside. Hell, if I was up there making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.200
myself a sandwich or grabbing another can of&nbsp;
Mountain Dew, I’d be a freaking puddle right now.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.080
It’s funny, my mom always told me&nbsp;
spending all day indoors was bad for me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.080
I’ll have to mention that to her, if she’s alive…

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.120
Point is, the world has gone to hell in a&nbsp;
handbasket and I’ve now got only one objective:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:49.200
Survive! I’m gonna see if I can survive the&nbsp;
horrifying, post-apocalyptic world of When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.720
Day Breaks! For this first day, I’m just gonna&nbsp;
hunker down. I kinda hope this is just a dream…&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:01.120
Day 2. Alright, I’m up and at ‘em, baby. Sadly,&nbsp;
I can now report that this isn’t a dream.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.720
This really is our horrible new reality. It’s&nbsp;
the sun’s world, and we’re just living in it!

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.440
I’ve been spending the last several hours just&nbsp;
waiting for nightfall outside. Against all odds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:15.760
the internet and the power grid haven’t gone&nbsp;
down yet - Guess whatever’s wrong with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.240
sun only affects people and animals, not&nbsp;
objects. Thank heaven for small mercies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.520
right? People on Twitter have been&nbsp;
live-posting their situations out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.320
sharing advice on how we might all be able to stay&nbsp;
out of the sun and survive this whole crazy thing.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.520
And hey, unless they’re dead or full of hot&nbsp;
air, maybe those “SCP Foundation” people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.760
know something about what’s going on here.&nbsp;
If we really can get to their buildings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.400
maybe we can figure out how to reverse all&nbsp;
this mess. Maybe. For now, I’m just gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.280
focus on staying alive. Hopefully night&nbsp;
hits soon, I really need the bathroom…

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.720
Oh boy, it’s Day Three and new issues are starting&nbsp;
to pop up. I’ve been heading upstairs to go to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:59.040
bathroom, but while I don’t want to be crude, I’m&nbsp;
running low on toilet paper and it’s starting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:04.560
become a problem. I ran out of my last roll a&nbsp;
few days ago, and now, I’m starting to go to my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.040
bookshelves. I had a few newspapers left that&nbsp;
I tore up and used for toilet paper first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.360
They weren’t exactly comfortable,&nbsp;
but hey, you need to make do.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.360
But without toilet paper and without&nbsp;
newspapers, I need to figure out what my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.040
favorite and least favorite books are.&nbsp;
I’m starting with the prefaces of all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.400
books - seeing as I don’t generally need to&nbsp;
re-read them. They’re expendable, you know?

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.960
A lot of these books I haven’t read since I&nbsp;
was fifteen, so maybe those’ll be the ones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.000
I can’t make my mind up on whether&nbsp;
I’m gonna use the Harry Potter books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.720
or the Percy Jackson and the Olympian books first.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:42.000
Uhhh, let me know what you think in the&nbsp;
comments, I guess. I need to go to the bathroom.&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.280
Day Four, and now, I’m trying to figure out&nbsp;
how to pass the time. As you already know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.920
I’m an avowed, life-long gamer. So, while&nbsp;
the electricity and the internet still work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.760
I’m gonna keep gaming to pass the time and keep&nbsp;
my all-too-precious sanity. I still have no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:03.360
intention of going out there, even at night,&nbsp;
but it’s left me feeling kind of stir-crazy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.240
I want to walk around the city again, I want to&nbsp;
go for a drive and feel the breeze in my hair.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:13.760
But seeing as I can’t do that without&nbsp;
experiencing a truly horrifying transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:19.280
I’ve been spending a lot of time on GTA V&nbsp;
online. Guess we’ll never get GTA 6 now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:25.360
what a bummer. Still, these last few days it’s&nbsp;
felt more like Los Santos has been my home than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.080
where I actually live. There were even a few other&nbsp;
people on the server. I don’t know about you, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.120
I take some comfort in that.
Hey folks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:40.240
I’m back, thankfully. Welcome to Day Five.
We’ve still got electricity, thankfully, hence why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.280
you can still see this. I haven’t heard anything&nbsp;
from my family. I don’t wanna assume the worst,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:51.280
but… It’s probably just best not to think about&nbsp;
it. I’ve been heading up and downstairs to grab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:55.440
more food at night. You’re probably wondering,&nbsp;
“But Kyle, why don’t you bring it all downstairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.880
to save going up altogether?” To which I’d say,&nbsp;
I don’t have a fridge downstairs, smart-ass.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.360
But I’m starting to realize food is gonna&nbsp;
be a real issue here. It’s kinda stupid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:12.960
now I think about it - In all the zombie movies&nbsp;
and TV shows I used to watch, it was all bullets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:17.440
and baseball bats. Killing your way through&nbsp;
all those undead freaks and worrying about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.440
rest later. Guess they don’t want&nbsp;
you to think about how you’re only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:28.000
ever a couple of weeks away from starvation, kinda&nbsp;
ruins the badass post-apocalypse power fantasy.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.960
I only have a couple days worth of food left&nbsp;
here, and after that, I’m gonna need to go out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:39.680
search for more. Or I’m gonna need to relocate.&nbsp;
I don’t feel comfortable here anymore, y’know?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:44.480
Early on, I thought when you got exposed to&nbsp;
the sunlight, it just killed you. But no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:49.920
it’s worse. You keep living, you’re&nbsp;
just changed into one of those… Things.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.440
These last few days, I’ve looked out the&nbsp;
windows when I’ve come up at night for food.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:59.680
I see them sometimes, slithering in the yard or&nbsp;
down the street, these things that used to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:03.520
people. I wonder if they’re people&nbsp;
that I knew once, before all this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:08.480
then I try to shove that thought out of my mind.&nbsp;
Freaking myself out about all this doesn’t help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:14.880
I know that much. I just keep thinking about&nbsp;
how they move, with this weird kind of… purpose?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.120
Like they’re searching for something,&nbsp;
but what could they be searching for?

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.400
I’m just gonna go and get more food. We’ll&nbsp;
speak again soon. Stay safe, whoever you are.&nbsp;

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.640
Welcome to Day 6. It’s nighttime now, and I’m&nbsp;
heading out for the first time. I keep seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:34.640
these weird, slimy creatures everywhere.&nbsp;
They make me kinda sick to look at them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:38.800
but I try my best to just keep moving. I’m&nbsp;
on a mission tonight: I’m gonna go to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:43.520
local supermarket and check if there’s still food&nbsp;
there, while also grabbing myself a quick snack.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:46.560
I’m gonna keep this one brief. I don’t&nbsp;
wanna do a full shopping spree tonight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.840
it’s already too late. I just&nbsp;
need to know the food is there.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:53.200
I decided earlier in the night, if I&nbsp;
survive this thing, and the getting is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:57.040
good at the Bargain Mart, I’ll make my way&nbsp;
back tomorrow for something a little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.400
substantial. After all, the fewer trips&nbsp;
I make out, the safer I’m likely to be.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.400
By the time I made it to the supermarket, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:08.320
while I was practically a nervous wreck from&nbsp;
the fear of turning into one of those things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:12.240
I made an amazing discovery: While the&nbsp;
windows were broken and the floor was a mess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:18.480
most of the food was still there.
Day 7. Or should I say, night seven. Galvanized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:22.720
by my success from the previous days, I decided to&nbsp;
come back to the supermarket with a shopping cart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.560
I wanted to get enough food for at least a&nbsp;
week, so I wouldn’t need to come back out again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.680
Hey, maybe I’m not so bad at this&nbsp;
whole apocalypse thing after all…

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:33.280
I grabbed plenty of canned food from the&nbsp;
supermarket. Most of the perishables had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:38.080
already gone moldy by the time I showed up, so&nbsp;
fresh fruit was out of the question. Suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.640
I started getting scared about&nbsp;
the thought of scurvy, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:44.240
pushed it quickly from my mind. I’d&nbsp;
cross that bridge when I got to it.&nbsp;

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:50.080
Hey hey, it’s Day 8, and I’m still kicking!&nbsp;
That’s gotta count for something, right?

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:54.320
I started taking more trips out at night&nbsp;
time, just to stretch my legs and keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:58.000
the blood flowing. When the slithering&nbsp;
things that were once people passed me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:03.840
I’d just make myself scarce and hide in the&nbsp;
shadows. I hear them muttering sometimes in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:10.320
melted voices. It’s unsettling, but it’s amazing&nbsp;
what you’ll get used to in just over a week.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.400
It’s eerie to see all these streets without&nbsp;
people in them. I know that I should probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:20.400
just stay inside, but I can’t. I don’t know if&nbsp;
this will ever just stop, and if it doesn’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:22.960
I don’t want to spend my last&nbsp;
days cooped up in my own basement.&nbsp;

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:28.560
Day 9. You know, there are some benefits&nbsp;
to being in the post-apocalypse, to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.480
ever so slightly offset all the utterly&nbsp;
crushing downsides. While, during the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:37.600
we’re all prisoners of the sun in our own homes,&nbsp;
at night, we can do whatever the hell we want.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:41.280
I took a baseball bat I keep in my closet&nbsp;
and went to a local furniture store.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:45.120
I smashed up every single vase and all the&nbsp;
windows, like one of those Rage Rooms, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.720
nobody could stop me. Then, afterwards,&nbsp;
I went straight to the local computer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:52.800
gaming store and took all the AlienWare&nbsp;
tech I could physically carry. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:56.800
no value in money anymore. If you want&nbsp;
something, you can just go and take it.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:01.920
Every cloud has a silver lining.
Day 10. More GTA V today. I decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.880
to get on my headset and speak to the&nbsp;
few others who were still around and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:09.840
on the servers. It was so nice to speak to&nbsp;
other human beings for once - They came from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:13.680
all over the world, and they were dealing&nbsp;
with this same “evil sun” insanity as me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:18.240
You have no idea how incredibly valuable it is&nbsp;
to find people to talk to in a time like this.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:21.600
The others players had plenty of theories as&nbsp;
to why all this had happened. Some thought it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.600
was some kind of mutant solar flare they’d&nbsp;
remembered reading about on some conspiracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:30.480
forum back in the day. Others speculated that it&nbsp;
was the result of some weapon created by the US or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:34.160
Russian or Chinese military that’d gone&nbsp;
wrong. One person said that maybe it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:38.240
a punishment from God, like maybe,&nbsp;
on some level, we all deserved it.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.560
Things got pretty quiet after that.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:44.880
Day 11. I’ve been having the most terrible&nbsp;
nightmares lately. It’s probably just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:48.560
product of all the stress I’ve been&nbsp;
under lately. But, in the nightmares,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:53.760
I’m running down a dark street, being chased&nbsp;
by those flesh creatures. I’m moving fast but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:59.040
they’re moving faster. They’re whispering to me&nbsp;
but I can’t make out anything they’re saying.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:04.080
This morning - which is to say, this evening&nbsp;
- I woke up screaming and drenched in sweat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:08.800
I can’t really explain why, but I feel like&nbsp;
something terrible is going to happen soon…&nbsp;

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:13.920
Okay, okay, I’m alive. That’s enough, isn’t&nbsp;
it? And if you’re watching this, I assume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:19.680
you’re alive too. Congratulations, Welcome to&nbsp;
the nightmare space between day 11 and day 27.&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:23.280
Sorry that I haven’t been in&nbsp;
contact for so long. As you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:29.040
I’m not at home anymore. You couldn’t pay me&nbsp;
to go back there - Ha. Not that money is worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:33.360
anything anymore. A lot’s happened since I&nbsp;
last made one of these, and I wish I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:39.040
tell you any of it was good. Hell, I wish I could&nbsp;
forget it all, but the things I’ve seen and heard…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:42.320
I don’t think they’re ever gonna&nbsp;
leave my head, no matter what I do.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:46.800
I thought about making another entry now and&nbsp;
then, but I always found a reason to put it off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:50.640
It’s remarkable how all your other priorities&nbsp;
fall away when you’re thinking about where your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:55.360
next meal is coming from, it just kind of&nbsp;
puts everything in perspective. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.360 --> 00:11:00.080
during my travels, I saw more of those&nbsp;
freaks slithering around. Sorry, sorry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:05.200
I know I shouldn’t call them that. It’s&nbsp;
kind of a coping mechanism, you know?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:09.440
It all gets a lot harder when you&nbsp;
have to think of them as… Ex-people.

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:12.400
That’s another thing all those&nbsp;
goofy zombie shows got wrong:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:17.040
It’s a lot harder to separate what&nbsp;
they were from what they are now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:20.480
especially when you know these were&nbsp;
your friends, your neighbors, your…

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:24.000
Well, I can’t avoid talking&nbsp;
about it forever, can I?

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.120
I stuffed my backpack with whatever I&nbsp;
could grab and left my home two nights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:31.120
ago. It wasn’t just because I was going&nbsp;
stir crazy back there, though I admit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:36.480
that didn’t exactly help. It was what happened&nbsp;
there. I’d just come back from a food run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:40.080
put most of it in the fridge, then retired&nbsp;
back down into the basement to enjoy a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:43.920
late-night snack and do a little gaming to&nbsp;
keep myself sane. I’d been doing everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:47.600
I could to reverse my circadian rhythms&nbsp;
and sleep during the day, just so I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:52.080
be fully operational during the twelve-hour&nbsp;
period that going outside wouldn’t melt me.

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:55.680
But just like all those stories&nbsp;
they told us when we were kids:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:59.520
There are monsters out there at&nbsp;
night, and they’re looking for us.

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:04.320
When I first heard the sound, I wondered if it was&nbsp;
something in-game, or maybe dripping from a leaky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:09.920
pipe. But no, it was too close to be fake and too&nbsp;
viscous to be water. That’s when I looked at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:15.760
door and saw this awful pink slime slithering in&nbsp;
underneath my door. It was one of those things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:21.840
those Ex-People, trying to get in. That’d be&nbsp;
bad enough, but then, it started talking to me…

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:29.760
“Kyle… My darling… Why are you all&nbsp;
cooped up down here? It isn’t healthy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:34.960
You ought to come out side,&nbsp;
sweetie, get some sun…”

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:44.160
It was my mom. Well, it used to be. Guess&nbsp;
she wanted to come over and visit me.

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:49.680
Needless to say, I got out of there, and I’ve&nbsp;
got no intention of going back. That place is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:55.920
dead to me now. I don’t ever want to think about&nbsp;
that voice ever again, both her and so… Not her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.920 --> 00:13:01.040
So now, I’m on the move, guess I’ll speak to&nbsp;
you again when I stop. Stay safe out there.&nbsp;

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:05.120
Day 28. I decided it was best to make&nbsp;
my way out of town, towards the fringes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:09.440
When day first broke, the people who were in the&nbsp;
most densely populated areas were the first to go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:13.280
That’s why I decided to hole up in a gas&nbsp;
station last night, just to avoid the sun.

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:19.120
But during the night, people came. Not&nbsp;
Ex-People, actual people. They showed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:23.520
in a Jeep outside the building, refueled,&nbsp;
and then came in. They were wearing black,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:29.120
cobbled-together outfits and hockey masks. They&nbsp;
were all either carrying bats or axes, too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:33.680
You can probably understand why I didn’t decide&nbsp;
to introduce myself when they busted their way in.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:36.800
I concealed myself in a broom&nbsp;
closet while they searched around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:41.120
It was nerve-shredding. I’d never been more&nbsp;
thankful in my life than when they left.&nbsp;

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:46.400
Day 29. Coming up on a month of this madness.&nbsp;
After the incident at the gas station,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:50.320
I realized I needed some kind of defense.&nbsp;
It’s not just the sun and those creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:53.920
I need to worry about. Just like the old&nbsp;
world, people could be dangerous here too.

00:13:53.920 --> 00:13:57.600
That’s why I snuck into a gun store in the&nbsp;
dead of night. Some of it had been looted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:02.080
but much like the supermarket near my home,&nbsp;
there was plenty still here. The walls were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:07.200
covered in all manner of rifles, shotguns, and&nbsp;
even submachine guns. I’d heard somewhere that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:12.320
revolvers are more reliable and easy to maintain&nbsp;
than other types, so since I’m a gun novice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:16.320
I grabbed a revolver and stuffed my pockets&nbsp;
with as many bullets as I could carry.

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:23.680
Let’s hope I never need to use any of them.
Day 30. Do I get to call this a month of survival?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:29.120
If we’re talking February, I’d be a month in&nbsp;
already. What a horribly dubious honor that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:34.320
is. I saw something disgusting last night and I&nbsp;
thought I’d share it just to get it off my mind.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:37.760
Last night, as I was moving through the&nbsp;
wilderness, I saw a group of other survivors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:42.720
gathered around the campfire. I remained scarce&nbsp;
but approached, just to see what was going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:46.880
still carrying my revolver, just to be&nbsp;
safe. But the people around the campfire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:52.800
were eating something, and when I saw&nbsp;
what they were eating, I almost threw up.&nbsp;

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:58.640
They were chopping up one of the Ex-People,&nbsp;
cooking the parts over the fire, and eating it.&nbsp;

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:04.800
Day 31, a month by anyone’s definition. Ever since&nbsp;
seeing those others eating one of the Ex-people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:10.400
I’ve had trouble eating even normal food&nbsp;
myself. My stomach aches and my throat burns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:16.160
I feel so weak. I keep laying down and resting.&nbsp;
I know I need to eat soon if I want to survive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:21.920
to day 32, but every time I think about eating,&nbsp;
I think about the gooey flesh of the Ex-People.

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:28.640
Sometimes, I wish I hadn’t survived this long.&nbsp;
I’ll eat soon. I just need to sleep first…&nbsp;

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:32.400
Day 32 to 43.
If you’ve lived this long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:37.520
you really ought to be proud of yourself.&nbsp;
I’ve seen thousands of those slimy Ex-People,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:41.440
and there are probably millions more&nbsp;
out there - Hell, maybe even billions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:46.240
if we’re being honest with ourselves here. Am&nbsp;
I just talking into the void here? Is there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:49.280
even anyone else out there who’s&nbsp;
human enough to watch this stuff?

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:54.000
Maybe I just need to keep thinking about&nbsp;
posterity. On the off-chance that the world ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:59.360
gets better, and we reach some time when children&nbsp;
are born again and all this fades from human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:04.960
memory, you’ll still have these stupid, pointless&nbsp;
little videos to remember how awful all this was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:11.120
That way, at least I can make myself&nbsp;
believe this all had some kind of point.

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:17.440
So, what’s happened? With me, not much. Still&nbsp;
moving at night, surviving, hiding in closets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:23.120
and underground parking complexes during&nbsp;
daylight. I’m down to my last few cans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:27.600
so I’m hoping to hit a supermarket&nbsp;
soon. What a ridiculous way to go:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:31.680
Starving in this new world, with so&nbsp;
many new, interesting ways to die.

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:37.680
With the Ex-People, things have been a little more&nbsp;
eventful. There used to be one blob to a person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:43.520
but they’ve started… Joining up? That’s the best&nbsp;
way I can put it. Things that used to be people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:49.440
and animals are starting to melt together, getting&nbsp;
bigger and bigger. They’ve never been aggressive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:53.360
but I think it’s best to stay out of&nbsp;
their way. Whatever all this is about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:57.840
I’m streetwise enough now to&nbsp;
know it can’t be anything good.

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:02.400
I’ll just keep moving, and I hope you can do&nbsp;
the same, whoever the hell you are. Hopefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:07.040
the next time I check in with you,&nbsp;
it’s with better news than this.

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:11.440
Day 44. I saw a shootout on the road&nbsp;
last night. The people who are left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:17.280
the ones who are still indeed people, are becoming&nbsp;
less human. Something about situations like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:23.120
this sustained stress and pain and hopelessness,&nbsp;
it weighs on you. There are no rules in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.120 --> 00:17:27.920
post-apocalypse, the only thing that can stop&nbsp;
you from doing anything is a bullet to the head.

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:32.000
Five or six people last night, as&nbsp;
afraid and desperate and hungry as me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:38.640
gunned each other down. They did this for reasons&nbsp;
I will never understand, even if I wanted to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:43.200
because there are no survivors left to tell&nbsp;
the tale. What a funny world we live in.&nbsp;

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:48.880
Day 45. I sleep when I can. It’s surreal, I&nbsp;
remember when I feared the dark and loved bright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:54.480
sunny days. Even all this time in, I still don’t&nbsp;
think I’m used to the switch being flipped.

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:57.520
I’ve been having awful dreams&nbsp;
again. I’m still running in them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.520 --> 00:18:02.560
with a deep red sun shining up in the sky.&nbsp;
I’m being chased by a mountain of flesh the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:07.040
size of Mount Everest. It’s swallowing&nbsp;
up the city behind me. It keeps getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:12.320
closer. No matter how fast I go, I just&nbsp;
can’t escape. It’ll get me eventually.

00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:16.480
Something terrible is going to&nbsp;
happen soon. I just know it.&nbsp;

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:22.080
Day 46. I shot a man today.&nbsp;
I don’t know if he survived;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:26.160
I hope he lived. We encountered each&nbsp;
other inside an abandoned building.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:31.200
I think we spooked each other and didn’t have&nbsp;
any time to ask if we were friends or foes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:36.240
We were too afraid either way. We both drew&nbsp;
our weapons, and I was faster than he was.

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:41.120
When my revolver discharged and he collapsed,&nbsp;
I ran off. Sun would come up in a few hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:45.920
and I just needed to find another place&nbsp;
to hide. What the hell have I become?

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:48.640
I don’t know how things could&nbsp;
get any worse than this.&nbsp;

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:52.240
Note to self, in the future,&nbsp;
don’t even dare to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:56.400
“How could things get any worse?” Because&nbsp;
if I’ve learned anything since this whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.400 --> 00:19:00.960
nightmare started, that is never a&nbsp;
rhetorical question. Welcome to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:07.840
space between Day 47 and Day 64. If you’re&nbsp;
still alive and watching this, I’m so sorry.

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:12.640
So, I’ve got good news and bad news.&nbsp;
I’ll give you the good news first:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:17.760
I’ve seen more people who haven’t been changed&nbsp;
yet. And the bad news? Last time I saw them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:21.920
they were being dragged out into the light,&nbsp;
kicking and screaming, in the tendrils of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:27.040
of those horrible flesh monsters I was telling&nbsp;
you about last time. They’ve gotten a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:33.760
bigger now, and when I told you they weren’t&nbsp;
aggressive, well, I spoke a little too soon.

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:38.960
I can’t just sleep during the day like I used to.&nbsp;
These monsters - and that’s what they are now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:45.680
they’re monsters, not people anymore - they&nbsp;
patrol, they hunt. They actively enter buildings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:50.240
searching for hiding places, searching for&nbsp;
people they can drag out into the light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:55.520
I’ve seen it with my own two eyes - The second&nbsp;
they’re out, they just start melting and fusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.520 --> 00:20:01.280
with the mass, making it even bigger, adding&nbsp;
another voice to the chorus. And I hate myself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:05.840
cause every time I’ve seen it happen, all&nbsp;
I can think is, “Thank god that’s not me.”

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:11.760
I wish I could do something to help, to save&nbsp;
them, but that’s not the world we live in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:16.080
The second they touch the light, it’s already&nbsp;
over. I wouldn’t be helping anyone by adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:20.800
my flesh to one of those things. I don’t&nbsp;
want them using my body to get other people.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:23.840
There’s only one thing I can&nbsp;
do now: Keep moving at night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.840 --> 00:20:28.960
stay hidden, get away from population centers.&nbsp;
I’ve realized where I need to go now: I’ve still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.960 --> 00:20:34.560
got a distant memory of those broadcasts in the&nbsp;
early days of the event. The SCP Foundation. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:39.200
noted down coordinates to the nearest facility&nbsp;
they had on the books - And if I’m honest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:45.440
“nearest” is only a relative term, cause at this&nbsp;
rate, it’s gonna take me an eternity to get there.

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:49.760
But it’ll be worth it in the end, when I get&nbsp;
there. It’ll all be worth it if I can at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:55.440
get some answers, at least know why the world&nbsp;
turned into this Hell. Those SCP folks seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.440 --> 00:21:00.480
better prepared for this than anyone, so even if&nbsp;
they can’t fix this, they’ve at least got to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:06.080
answers, right? Somebody needs to have&nbsp;
answers. I really want to believe that.

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:09.760
When the sun goes down, I’ll start&nbsp;
moving again. If you’re watching this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:14.320
wish me luck. I don’t have much&nbsp;
food left. I’ll do what I can.

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:21.040
Yeah, I realize I’m not looking great right&nbsp;
now, but trust me, you should see the other guy.

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:29.840
Day 65 to Day 86. Never thought I’d make it&nbsp;
this far, but life’s just full of surprises.

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:34.560
Before you ask - and I mean, why would&nbsp;
you ask? It’s not like I can hear you - it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:40.800
wasn’t one of the monsters that did this to me,&nbsp;
it was another person, just like me. Desperate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:45.920
hungry, afraid. The one difference between&nbsp;
me and them was the fact they had a handgun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.920 --> 00:21:50.720
and I didn’t. They asked for all the food I&nbsp;
had, and when I wasn’t exactly forthcoming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:56.240
they decided to shoot me, and steal the last&nbsp;
of my food while I lay bleeding on the ground.

00:21:56.880 --> 00:22:03.600
Well, that’s not entirely fair. They did leave&nbsp;
me with one protein bar, which I had to cave and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:08.640
eat a couple of days ago. Since then, I’ve just&nbsp;
been foraging what little food I can from plants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:14.240
along the way during my nighttime walks. But&nbsp;
it isn’t much, and my wound is giving me grief.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:21.440
I sure hope they’ve got doctors at this&nbsp;
SCP Foundation, or otherwise, I may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:24.000
even more out of luck than I thought.

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:29.440
Here’s the good news for you, since I know&nbsp;
how much you love that. I’m not far off of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:34.480
the Foundation site now. Even in this state, I’m&nbsp;
probably only a couple of weeks away. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.480 --> 00:22:40.480
maybe I can will myself to live that long,&nbsp;
at least. If I can get some answers just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:46.320
before I die, then I can die happy. And&nbsp;
sometimes, folks, that’s all you can ask for.

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:54.640
Final stretch. Let’s hope I see you&nbsp;
again on the other end. Stay safe…&nbsp;

00:22:56.320 --> 00:23:02.640
But… I don’t understand. Where is everybody?&nbsp;
Hello? Is anybody there? God damn it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:06.720
why is nobody here? I don’t understand.&nbsp;
They were meant to have the answers. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:11.280
were meant to know what was going on here,&nbsp;
but they’re all gone too. It isn’t fair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:17.440
it isn’t fair. Is this it? Is it all just over?&nbsp;
The end of the freaking world as we know it?

00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:19.200
It isn’t fair, it isn’t fair, it isn’t fair…

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:26.640
Wait… Are those footsteps? Hello? Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:29.840
hello, I’m over here! Who’s there?
Oh my god, what the hell is that thing!?&nbsp;

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:34.640
No, no, get away from me, oh god!
“Disgusting…”

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:42.560
Now go check out “When Day Breaks SCP-096”&nbsp;
and “When Day Breaks SCP-049” for more tales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.560 --> 00:23:47.760
from the nightmarish universe&nbsp;
of SCP-001 - When Day Breaks!

